me>Perrone</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Adolescents carrying a missense mutation in the CART gene exhibit increased anxiety and depression</article-title><source>Depress Anxiety</source><year>2006</year><volume>23</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>90</fpage><lpage>92</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16400624</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b14-ndt-5-207"><label>14.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Klein</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Walsh</surname><given-names>BT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Eating disorders: clinical features and pathophysiology</article-title><source>Physiol Behav</source><year>2004</year><volume>81</volume><fpage>359</fpage><lpage>374</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15159176</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b15-ndt-5-207"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="web"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>ISTAT (Italian National Institute of Statistics)</collab></person-group><year>2005</year>Cited on January 10, 2009. Available from <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.istat.it/">http://www.istat.it/</ext-link></citation></ref><ref id="b16-ndt-5-207"><label>16.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Speranza</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Corcos</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Loas</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Depressive personality dimensions and alexithymia in eating disorders</article-title><source>Psychiatry Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>135</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>153</fpage><lpage>163</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15913785</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b17-ndt-5-207"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Marcus</surname><given-names>MD</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalarchian</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Binge eating in children and adolescents</article-title><source>Int J Eat Disord</source><year>2003</year><volume>34</volume><issue>Suppl</issue><fpage>S47</fpage><lpage>S57</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12900986</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b18-ndt-5-207"><label>18.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ramacciotti</surname><given-names>CE</given-names></name><name><surname>Coli</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Passaglia</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Lacorte</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pea</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Dell&#x02019;Osso</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Binge eating disorder: prevalence and psychopathological features in a clinical sample of obese people in Italy</article-title><source>Psychiatry Res</source><year>2000</year><volume>94</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>131</fpage><lpage>138</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10808038</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b19-ndt-5-207"><label>19.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gual</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>P&#x000e9;rez-Gaspar</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mart&#x000ed;nez-Gonz&#x000e1;lez</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Lahortiga</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>de Irala-Est&#x000e9;vez</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Cervera-Enguix</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Self-esteem, personality, and eating disorders: baseline assessment of a prospective population-based cohort</article-title><source>Int J Eat Disord</source><year>2002</year><volume>31</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>261</fpage><lpage>273</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11920987</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b20-ndt-5-207"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tripp</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Cockett</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Parents, parenting, and family breakdown</article-title><source>Arch Dis Child</source><year>1998</year><volume>78</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>104</fpage><lpage>108</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9579148</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><table-wrap id="t1-ndt-5-207" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Psychiatric symptoms and pathologic values</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>AN (n 65)</bold></th><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>BN (n 16)</bold></th><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>BED (n 14)</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A2 (Social anxiety)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60 (50&#x02013;71)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54 (44&#x02013;73)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57 (43&#x02013;73)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D2 (Anhedony and indifference)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56 (46&#x02013;71)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62 (48&#x02013;76)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">49 (46&#x02013;58)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D4 (Sense of inadequacy, low self esteem)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (49&#x02013;73)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62 (53&#x02013;72)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">56 (47&#x02013;67)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P1 (Bulimic behavior)<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-ndt-5-207">*</xref></td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">42 (42&#x02013;50)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">65 (44&#x02013;73)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61 (52&#x02013;72)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P3 (Acceptance and evaluation of one&#x02019;s own body)<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-ndt-5-207">*</xref></td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">47 (42&#x02013;61)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57 (35&#x02013;70)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">70 (65&#x02013;71)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P2P3 (Anorexic behavior + acceptance and evaluation of one&#x02019;s own body)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">55 (46&#x02013;68)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61 (50&#x02013;70)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">61 (55&#x02013;69)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-ndt-5-207"><p><bold>Notes:</bold> The table shows median (IQR) values of pathologic subscales in the three groups;</p></fn><fn id="tfn2-ndt-5-207"><label>*</label><p>p &#x0003c; 0.0001.</p></fn><fn id="tfn3-ndt-5-207"><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> AN, anorexia nervosa; BED, binge-eating disorder; BN, bulimia nervosa.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-ndt-5-207" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Pathologic profiles involving parental separation; anorexia and binge-eating disorder (BED) (last value)</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Anorexia</bold></th><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Separated (n 9)</bold></th><th valign="middle" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Nonseparated (n 56)</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A2 (Social anxiety)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">73<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn5-ndt-5-207">*</xref> (55&#x02013;75)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57 (49&#x02013;70)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A4 (School-Related anxiety)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn5-ndt-5-207">*</xref> (50&#x02013;57)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">46 (39&#x02013;53)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D (Depression)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">71 (51&#x02013;75)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (47&#x02013;70)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D1 (Depressed mood)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62 (49&#x02013;73)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">59 (47&#x02013;68)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D2 (Anhedony and indifference)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">62 (48&#x02013;76)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">52 (46&#x02013;69)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D4 (Sense of inadequacy and low self esteem)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">66 (47&#x02013;74)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57 (49&#x02013;69)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">D7 (Hopelessness)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">67 (45&#x02013;74)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">51 (42&#x02013;65)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P2 (Anorexic behavior)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">65 (46&#x02013;70)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (45&#x02013;68)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P4 (Psychological aspects)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60 (43&#x02013;69)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57 (45&#x02013;65)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P2P3 (Anorexic behavior + acceptance and evaluation of one&#x02019;s own body)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60 (46&#x02013;72)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54 (45&#x02013;67)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BED</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Separated (n.5)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nonseparated (n.9)</td></tr><tr><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">P (Psychogenic eating disorders)<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn5-ndt-5-207">*</xref></td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">69 (70&#x02013;52)</td><td valign="top" align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">54 (51&#x02013;61)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn4-ndt-5-207"><p><bold>Notes:</bold> Anorexic and BED patients with parental separation show higher values in specific subscales;</p></fn><fn id="tfn5-ndt-5-207"><label>*</label><p>p &#x0003c; 0.05 vs nonseparated group.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t3-ndt-5-207" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Influence of sex over anorexia</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th valign="middle" align="left" 